## Per-drawer photo uploads

- Select/preview the intended drawer, touch its image (or **New image of this drawer**), confirm, then choose a local PNG/JPG. Google Photos HTML/URLs are not uploaded; download the actual photo first. The displayed drawer number is captured before the file picker opens, so changing selection during upload never redirects the upload to another drawer.
- Drawers without an image show the clickable [dummy.jpg](./html/images/dummy.jpg) placeholder. Failed image loads also fall back to this placeholder with an explicit error message; the stored photo reference is not silently replaced.
- The browser resizes to at most 1600 pixels on the longest side without upscaling or cropping, preserves aspect ratio, and re-encodes without the original metadata. PNG stays PNG if it fits; otherwise JPEG compression is used with a white transparency background and further downscaling as needed. Stored images are limited to **1 MiB (1,048,576 bytes)**. Original files over 40 MiB or 40 megapixels are rejected explicitly.
- Uploads use `POST /api/drawers/101/photo` with the original `basePhoto`, PNG/JPEG MIME type and base64 content. The server validates path, image signature and size; text PATCH cannot edit photo paths. Concurrent replacement of the same drawer's photo returns a conflict. Text edits and pending autosave drafts remain untouched.
- Images get unique `html/images/drawer_101_<random-id>.jpg` (or `.png`) names. Only drawer 101's photo path changes; other drawers sharing the old photo retain it. After a successful replacement, the old display image is deleted only if no drawer references it. Full-resolution originals in `photos` and `dummy.jpg` are never deleted. Local cleanup runs inside the inventory write queue. Cleanup failures show a warning without claiming the successfully saved new photo failed. This is replacement-triggered cleanup, not a scan deleting existing unreferenced files.
- Cloudflare writes the image into GitHub, then updates the inventory with bounded SHA-race retries, under the existing Access/same-origin protection. These are two GitHub commits, plus a cleanup commit when needed: if the inventory update fails, the new file may remain unreferenced, but the UI reports failure and never claims the drawer was updated. Cleanup re-reads current references and deletes using the old image SHA. Avoid restoring old image references through external repository edits during uploads; those edits cannot participate in this check. A network timeout can leave save completion uncertain; Reload to inspect before retrying.
- Newly uploaded images are read immediately through `/api/photos/<filename>` from local disk or GitHub (authenticated on Cloudflare), without waiting for an asset deployment. Filename allowlists prevent arbitrary repository/file access. Errors are visible, uploads are never automatically retried, and Reload is disabled while an upload is in progress.

## Direct USB printing test (M2-H)

- Run `npm ci`, then `npm start`, and open http://127.0.0.1:8787 in desktop Chrome or Edge. The same controls work on the HTTPS Cloudflare site after deployment; printing runs entirely in the browser, not the server or Worker.
- Close the official Niimbot app and other printer tabs first. Select drawers and click **Print selected labels**: it connects if necessary and prints immediately, without an application confirmation dialog. A single already-authorized matching M2-H USB serial device is reused, including after refresh; the browser's device chooser appears only when authorization is missing or multiple matching devices require a choice. Browser permission prompts cannot be bypassed. **Connect USB printer** remains optional and does not print. No replacement driver or local print bridge is needed. Firefox and Safari do not support this Web Serial workflow.
- Load **50 x 30 mm gap labels** with the 50 mm edge across the head. The workflow is restricted to M2-H, 300 DPI, B1 print task, density 3, black-and-white printing. It never prints on page load, selection or connect, and never automatically retries.
- Selection is snapshotted in inventory order, including hidden selections and unsaved edits, without saving inventory or requiring the editing PIN. PDF export is unchanged.
- The 567-dot head cannot cover the full 50 mm width. The raster uses a byte-aligned **560 x 354 pixels** (approximately 47.41 x 29.97 mm), centered within the existing 50 x 30 mm design. Only about 1.29 mm of white margin on each side is cropped; text/artwork remains at its original scale and within the existing 1.5 mm clipping margin. Check actual roll orientation and alignment before a larger batch.
- Printing waits for the protocol's completion status and reports errors, including print-end failures. After any failure, some labels may already have printed; inspect the printer before retrying. Controls are disabled during connection/printing to prevent overlapping jobs. **Disconnect printer** releases the port for the official app.
- Open browser developer tools to see `NIIMBOT TX` / `NIIMBOT RX` packet logs. No separate COM monitor opens the port. These logs stay local.
- The persistent USB presence indicator checks already-authorized matching devices on page load and when returning to the tab, and listens for Web Serial plug/unplug events even before opening a print connection. Unplugging shows a red warning independently of print-job messages; reconnecting restores presence. This never opens a port, sends printer commands, or asks permission. Without prior authorization, "absent or not authorized" cannot distinguish the two; use Connect/Print once to authorize. Browsers expose USB IDs, not Windows COM names: COM6 was verified on the current PC, but is not hard-coded or falsely displayed as browser-detected.
- [label-print.js](./html/label-print.js) uses MIT-licensed `@mmote/niimbluelib`, pinned in [package.json](./package.json) and bundled locally by the existing label build. Both bundles are generated before local startup, tests and Cloudflare deployment; no CDN or security-policy relaxation is needed.
- USB model-information communication was verified on this PC, and the user confirmed successful physical label printing. The one-click flow is validated with mocked browser transport, without consuming labels automatically.

## Cloudflare hosted editor

The site is hosted at https://niimbot-labels.johan-351.workers.dev/. The initial deployment was read-only; [worker.mjs](./worker.mjs) now reads current inventory from GitHub and saves edits through GitHub's Contents API. No D1 database is used. The local editor continues to read/write the local JSON file independently.

- Worker name: `niimbot-labels`; production branch: `main`; repository root is the build root.
- Install dependencies: `npm ci`.
- Build command in Cloudflare: `npm run build:cloudflare`.
- Deploy command: `npm run deploy`.
- Preview branch deploy command, if offered: `npx wrangler versions upload`.
- Local Worker preview: `npm run dev:cloudflare`. Ordinary local editing still uses `npm start`.
- The Worker preview requires a valid Access JWT and GitHub credentials for API calls; it does not bypass authentication in development. Automated tests use generated signing keys and an isolated mock repository.
- Configuration is in [wrangler.jsonc](./wrangler.jsonc); only [html](./html) is uploaded as public static assets. The repository, full-resolution originals, local server and credentials are not public static files.
- GET `/api/drawers` reads GitHub's current `drawers.json` on `main`, without returning a stale deployed snapshot on failure. PATCH `/api/drawers/101` (etc.) commits only title, description, remark and searchTag changes, preserving exact spaces, Unicode and newlines.
- All traffic is protected by the Worker Access application `niimbot-labels - Cloudflare Workers`, using the dedicated `niimbot-labels-owner` Allow policy with the owner's exact email. This intentionally requires login even for viewing. Do not disable Access or add broader Allow/Bypass policies when remote editing is enabled.

## Hosted authentication and saving configuration

Cloudflare Access protects the static site at the edge. The Worker also verifies the `Cf-Access-Jwt-Assertion` cryptographically with `jose`, checking RS256 signature, issuer, audience, expiration, issued-at and subject, and requiring an authenticated user's email. Fake or expired headers are rejected before accessing GitHub. PATCH requests additionally require the site's exact Origin. Access key rotation is handled through the team's remote JWK set.

- Runtime secret: `GITHUB_TOKEN`, set in **Worker Settings > Runtime variables and secrets > Production**, not in build variables. Use a fine-grained GitHub token restricted to `jeedee23/Niimbot-labels` with Contents Read and write; renew it before expiration. Never put the value in browser code, Git, screenshots or chat.
- Public configuration in [wrangler.jsonc](./wrangler.jsonc): `ACCESS_ISSUER`, `ACCESS_AUD`, `GITHUB_REPOSITORY`, `GITHUB_BRANCH`. The Access issuer is `https://meam-mpms.cloudflareaccess.com`; the AUD was verified from the application's Additional settings > AUD tag. These identifiers are not login tokens or secrets.
- Each patch includes original values in `base` and new values in `changes`. Before writing, the Worker rechecks those values against the latest file and commits using GitHub's file SHA. A SHA race retries at most three times, re-reading and rechecking fields each time. Same-field conflicts return 409 instead of overwriting another writer. GitHub errors are surfaced explicitly; no credentials or upstream error bodies are returned to the browser.
- Network timeout can leave the outcome uncertain if GitHub completed the write but its response was lost. Export unsaved drafts and reload to check saved values before retrying; a stale base may correctly trigger a conflict.
- In **Worker Settings > Builds > Build watch paths**, keep Include `*` and add Exclude `drawers.json`. This is a required dashboard setup step before routine editing to prevent each inventory commit from rebuilding the static site. Code/photo changes should still deploy; the Worker always reads live JSON, so inventory changes do not need a deployment.
- Hosted edits update GitHub, not the local disk. Do not edit a stale local JSON and push over hosted changes. Synchronize Git first, or export/reconcile drafts if the local inventory also has edits.
- Verification: 44 automated tests pass, covering local save behavior, label PDF output, valid/missing/forged/expired/wrong-audience Access JWTs, exact text preservation, bounded SHA races, same-field conflicts, provider failures, Cloudflare-compatible redirect handling and unchanged static-asset forwarding. The deployment bundle was checked with Wrangler's dry-run.
- Deployment troubleshooting: Cloudflare Workers supports fetch redirect modes `follow` and `manual`, not `error`. GitHub requests use `manual`; any redirect response is rejected, not followed with credentials. A real local Workers-runtime probe reproduced the original TypeError with `error` and returned GitHub HTTP 200 with `manual`. Loading failures explicitly say that no save was attempted; only failed write requests warn about uncertain save outcomes.

## Autopilot notes

Non-blocking questions and uncertainties are documented here and in drawer remarks rather than interrupting recovery. The user-provided [commit-and-push.ps1](../githubcommitall/ps_scripts/commit-and-push.ps1) was run with a dedicated one-repository workspace and session-local configuration to avoid committing unrelated workspace changes. The script also publishes its own run log in the githubcommitall repository and adds timestamp comments to changed source files.

Validation performed during recovery:
- 11 automated inventory/API tests cover complete numbering, all 12 photo links, approved records, exact whitespace/newline/tag persistence, restart-readable JSON, invalid input, localhost restrictions, atomic file cleanup and concurrent writes.
- Browser checks used a disposable inventory copy, not the real data. Search, photo selection and nonexistent-drawer display passed.
- With a controlled browser clock, no save occurred at 4,999 ms; typing a space reset the timer, and saving occurred at 5,000 ms after that last input. Leading, repeated and trailing spaces and cursor position were preserved.
- A delayed save response did not overwrite newer typing. The follow-up save preserved newline-containing descriptions and spaces/empty entries in comma-separated tags.
- A simulated network failure retained drafts and Save now retried successfully. An externally changed title produced HTTP 409, retaining both the other writer's saved value and this browser's unsaved draft; JSON export remained available.

Backup/setup blockers recorded on 2026-11-01:
- The script's dry run passed, but the actual run stopped in timestamp processing: PowerShell `Get-Item` does not find hidden `.gitignore` on Linux without `-Force`. The backup tool itself has not been changed. Native Git is used to commit this recovery locally, including `.gitignore`, so it will no longer be an uncommitted file on the next script run.
- GitHub HTTPS push cannot proceed in this session: `gh auth status` reports no logged-in hosts, and Git reports that it cannot obtain a password. An SSH probe was also blocked by a missing trusted GitHub host key; host verification was not bypassed. Remote backup is therefore still pending.
- The backup script did create a local commit for its own run log in githubcommitall, but that push failed for the same authentication reason.
- After authenticating locally (for example with `gh auth login` and `gh auth setup-git`), rerun the usual commit-and-push workflow to publish both repositories' pending commits. No credentials have been placed in the application or committed files.
- The local editor was started and verified at http://127.0.0.1:8787. Browser tests used a separate disposable JSON file, so the recovered inventory retains its original transcriptions.